cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "transport-asset: a content-addressed casync chunk CDN"
abstract: >
  Chunks, addressed by their content, served to whoever is allowed to ask. A
  transport layer is the input that triggers an interactor, and this one really is a
  server: an h2o loop with handlers, answering requests. That is what separates it
  from interactor-authority, which had an h2o loop and no handlers and turned out to
  be an interactor all along. It is an edge that needs no ring, so it lands wherever
  it likes.
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 0.1.0-dev.1
date-released: "2026-08-17"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/transport-asset"
keywords:
  - content-addressed-storage
  - casync
  - cdn
  - h2o
references:

  - type: software
    title: "h2o: an optimized HTTP server with support for HTTP/1.x, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3"
    url: "https://github.com/h2o/h2o"
    notes: >
      Required outright as a system library rather than vendored, the way
      datasource-store requires its own. The loop and the handlers are this
      repository's; the server is not.

  - type: software
    title: "casync: content-addressable data synchronizer"
    authors:
      - family-names: Poettering
        given-names: Lennart
    url: "https://github.com/systemd/casync"
    notes: >
      The chunking and addressing scheme the served objects are in. `casync_fetch_chunk`
      is the single call this makes onto idtxcli in datasource-flow -- one function
      rather than a dependency, because fetch and authorization do not care which
      backing store answers.

  - type: software
    title: "entities-lean-rebac: the relationship-based access-control hexagon"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/entities-lean-rebac"
    notes: >
      "Whoever is allowed to ask" is decided by gen/rebac.{c,h}, which is emitted from
      that Lean core and vendored here rather than fetched. A copy can drift from the
      Lean that produced it, and saying where it came from is what makes the drift
      findable.
